			<section class="faq" id="faq">
				<div class="container">
					<div class="section-head">
						<h2>Questions</h2>
						<p>The ones people actually ask.</p>
					</div>
					<div class="faq-list">
						<details>
							<summary>Does it work with iPhone?</summary>
							<p>
								Not currently. Mac Share speaks the Android Quick Share protocol.
								iPhone support would need a different transport, which isn't planned.
							</p>
						</details>
						<details>
							<summary>What about Windows and Linux?</summary>
							<p>
								Not yet — Mac Share is macOS-only today. The protocol is open and
								documented, so a port is possible.
							</p>
						</details>
						<details>
							<summary>How fast are transfers?</summary>
							<p>
								As fast as your Wi-Fi. On the same network, expect 10–60 MB/s — no
								internet round-trip and no upload limits.
							</p>
						</details>
						<details>
							<summary>Is it secure?</summary>
							<p>
								Transfers are PIN-confirmed and encrypted, and stay on your local
								network. Nothing is uploaded to a server.
							</p>
						</details>
						<details>
							<summary>Where do received files go?</summary>
							<p>Downloads by default. You can point it at any folder in Settings.</p>
						</details>
						<details>
							<summary>Is it really free?</summary>
							<p>
								Yes. Mac Share is open source under the MIT license. There's no paid
								tier and no telemetry.
							</p>
						</details>
					</div>
				</div>
			</section>

